 ### BrightDreamer
-BrightDreamer is a method predicting the deviation of a set of fixed anchor points to determine the centers of Gaussians [S1]. The deviation of a set of fixed anchor points is predicted, and the centers of Gaussians are determined from these deviations [S1].
-
-### NeRF-Guided 3D Gaussian Splatting
-NeRF-Guided 3D Gaussian Splatting uses a pretrained NeRF as a teacher to guide feature distillation, improving alignment between geometry and semantics [S3, unofficial].
+Predicts the deviation of a set of fixed anchor points to determine the centers of Gaussians [S1].
 
 ### Defocus Blur Modeling
-Defocus Blur Modeling models optical defocus by adjusting the scale $s$ of 3D Gaussians based on their distance from the camera's focal plane [S4]. During training, use larger scale values $\bar{s}_j$ to rasterize blurred images. During testing, use smaller scale values $s_j$ to render sharp details. The scale $s$ (scale of a 3D Gaussian) controls dispersion; larger $s$ models out-of-focus points, smaller $s$ models in-focus points. Unlike a naive Gaussian kernel that blurs the entire image uniformly, this method selectively enlarges covariances only for Gaussians corresponding to blurred regions [S4].
-
-### GauS (Gaussian Splatting Surface Reconstruction)
-GauS is a render-then-fuse approach for high-fidelity mesh reconstruction from 3DGS inputs without fine-tuning. It uses volumetric fusion to convert 3D Gaussians into textured triangle meshes. It is a plug-and-play module compatible with various GS-based methods. Unlike NeRF-based re-training or further fine-tuning required for explicit surface modeling in other methods, GauS converts optimized Gaussians directly into textured meshes serving as a versatile plug-and-play solution [S5, unofficial].
-
-### Hybrid Scene Representation (Foreground/Background)
-A scene representation comprising Gaussian foreground models and background models. The background models support diverse backends like environment maps, textured meshes, and neural representations. Specifically, sky background is built using spherical environment maps composed of Gaussians to reduce artifacts in unbounded outdoor scenes [S5, unofficial].
-
-### Barycentric Splat Parameterization
-Barycentric Splat Parameterization is a method to constrain 2D Gaussian Splat centers to lie on triangular mesh faces using barycentric coordinates, allowing splats to move freely on the face while preserving per-splat attributes. For each mesh face with vertices $v_1, v_2, v_3$, compute the splat center $m$ using the barycentric coordinates $u, w$:
-$$ m = u \cdot v_1 + w \cdot v_2 + (1 - u - w) \cdot v_3 $$
-where $m$ is the 2DGS mean (center), $v_1, v_2, v_3$ are the vertices of the triangular mesh face, and $u, w \in [0, 1]$ with $u + w \le 1$ are the barycentric coordinates [S6].
-
-### In-Plane Rotation for Splats
-In-Plane Rotation for Splats is a parameterization for the orientation of Gaussian Splats on the face plane using an in-plane rotation parameter $R_{2d}$, transformed into world space:
-$$ R = R_s \cdot R_{2d} $$
-where $R$ is the final rotation matrix used for rasterization, $R_s$ is the rotation matrix of the triangle faces, and $R_{2d}$ is the in-plane rotation parameter [S6].
+Models optical defocus by adjusting the scale $s$ of 3D Gaussians based on their distance from the camera's focal plane [S6]. Larger $s$ models out-of-focus points, smaller $s$ models in-focus points [S6].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward inference is significantly faster than iterative optimization methods (like standard 3DGS) as it avoids per-scene gradient descent. The complexity is dominated by the sparse 3D CNN feature extraction and the MLP head evaluations [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the global point cloud $\mathcal{P}$ and the sparse feature volume $\mathcal{F}$. The downsampling of initial primitives reduces computation compared to dense pixel-aligned methods, but the global volume approach may consume more memory than local cost-volume methods [S2].
-*   **Measured Performance:** EVolSplat achieves real-time rendering speeds suitable for urban scene reconstruction [S2]. It improves global consistency and reduces ghosting artifacts compared to pixel-aligned methods like MVSplat, Flash3D, and DrivingForward [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Global consistency, reduced ghosting artifacts, efficient feed-forward prediction, handles foreground/background separation effectively [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of monocular depth estimators; noisy depth can lead to initialization errors requiring filtering [S2]. May lose high-frequency appearance details due to downsampling, necessitating image-based rendering for color [S2].
-*   **When to use:** Urban scene reconstruction, autonomous driving applications, and scenarios requiring fast, consistent novel view synthesis from sparse inputs [S2].
-*   **When not to use:** Small-scale scenes with high overlap where iterative optimization is feasible and higher fidelity is required without the overhead of global volume construction [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward methods (e.g., EVolSplat, GRM) are significantly faster than iterative optimization, enabling real-time rendering [S5].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Global volume methods require storing a 3D feature volume, which can be large for urban scenes [S5]. Sequential decoders reduce memory by specializing decoders [S2].
+*   **Performance:** EVolSplat achieves real-time rendering for urban scenes [S5]. GRM enables fast 3D-aware generation [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time inference, generalizability across scenes, reduced ghosting artifacts compared to pixel-aligned methods [S5].
+*   **Limitations:** Dependency on depth estimator quality [S5], potential loss of high-frequency details if primitives are downsampled [S5].
 
 ## Background
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction, specifically as implemented in EVolSplat, was introduced by Sheng Miao, Jiaxin Huang, Dongfeng Bai, Xu Yan, Hongyu Zhou, Yue Wang, Bingbing Liu, Andreas Geiger, and Yiyi Liao from Zhejiang University, Huawei Noah’s Ark Lab, and the University of Tübingen [S2]. The method was published in CVPR 2025 [S2]. It addresses the limitations of existing feed-forward 3DGS methods that suffer from multi-view inconsistencies and duplicated content by predicting 3D Gaussians across multiple frames within a unified volume using a 3D convolutional network [S2].
+Originated from the need to accelerate 3D Gaussian Splatting reconstruction, which traditionally requires slow per-scene optimization [S5]. EVolSplat was introduced in 2025 for efficient urban view synthesis [S5]. GRM and sequential decoding approaches were developed for 3D-aware generative models [S2]. BrightDreamer predicts anchor deviations for 3D generation [S1].
 
